# Regression pin for #1417: a string whose text is a YAML scalar of another type must be QUOTED
# on dump, or it changes type on the way back in. `safe_dump("true")` emitted the bare scalar
# `true`, which `safe_load` then read as the BOOLEAN — silent type corruption across a
# serialization boundary. PyYAML quotes all of these, and now so does Sharpy.
#
# The rule is asked of YamlScalarResolver, the same authority safe_load resolves through, so the
# two directions cannot disagree by construction.
#
# The control — that `hello` stays BARE, gaining nothing — is the third cell, and it is here
# because #1348 landed. It could not live in this fixture before: this fixture is compared against
# CPython by the differential oracle, and PyYAML terminates a PLAIN scalar document with a `...`
# marker that Sharpy did not emit, so CPython printed `hello\n...` where Sharpy printed `hello`
# and the control failed on #1348's account rather than this one's. Both arms now agree, which is
# exactly the condition the earlier header set for moving it here.
#
# It matters that the control is BOTH lines: `hello` bare says the quoting rule did not overreach,
# and `...` says an unquoted scalar takes the plain-document path that the quoted cells above do
# not. If #1417's converter ever quoted `hello`, the marker would vanish with the bareness and
# this fixture would fail twice over.
import yaml

def main():
    print(yaml.safe_dump("true").strip())
    print(yaml.safe_dump("42").strip())
    print(yaml.safe_dump("hello").strip())
